  "measures": [],
  "notes": "Optical tweezers as in a biophysics lab: 100 mW of 1064 nm light filling a water-immersion objective of NA 1.2 (drawn as an ideal lens inside the water, f = 2 mm), holding a 120 nm polystyrene bead in water. Select the screen at the focus and open its Trap tab: the bead sits at the focus with trap frequencies of a few hundred kHz across the beam (about 300 kHz along the light’s polarisation, p in the plane, and 430 kHz across it: a steep focus is wider along its polarisation) and about 150 kHz along it. The water is on the bench, so the trap takes its index and its viscosity: the damping is far faster than the trap frequencies (Q well under ½), so the bead does not oscillate but diffuses in the trap, as it does in real tweezers. Try a silica bead, a smaller one, or a lower NA (a longer f in the objective’s formula) and watch the radiation pressure win.",
